+### CUDAVolumetricRender
+A hardware-accelerated volumetric rendering function provided by CUDALink for CUDA-capable graphics cards, performing real volume rendering [S3, unofficial]. It differs from polygon-based methods by being a "real deal" volumetric renderer with a transfer function, unlike the polygon-based method which mimics the effect [S3, unofficial]. The steps involve ensuring the latest CUDA paclet is installed, importing volumetric slice data, converting slices to a packed integer array with depth 3 (elements 0-255), repartitioning the data dimensions from $d \times h \times w$ to $h \times w \times d$ without changing data order, and calling `CUDAVolumetricRender` with the prepared data [S3, unofficial]. A pitfall is that the function requires specific repartitioning (not transposition) and may throw memory allocation errors [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Hardware-accelerated volume rendering emerged as graphics hardware capabilities evolved from fixed-function pipelines to programmable shaders. Early implementations relied on texture mapping hardware to perform 3D texture lookups and interpolation, effectively offloading the sampling step [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. As GPUs became programmable (e.g., DirectX 9 level architectures), multi-pass stream models and single-pass fragment shader ray casting became dominant, allowing for complex shading, early ray termination, and adaptive sampling [S1, unofficial]. The technique is widely used in medical imaging, scientific visualization, and real-time interactive applications where performance is critical [S1, unofficial][S2].
+Hardware-accelerated volume rendering emerged as GPUs evolved from fixed-function pipelines to programmable architectures (e.g., DirectX 9, OpenGL Shading Language). Early methods relied on 3D texture mapping hardware to perform resampling, as seen in the SGI Reality Engine [S2]. Later, the advent of fragment shaders allowed for more flexible ray marching implementations, such as the multi-pass stream model on ATI 9700 hardware [S1, unofficial]. The technique has become the standard for real-time volume visualization in medical imaging and scientific visualization, enabling interactive exploration of large 3D datasets [S1, unofficial][S2][S3, unofficial].
 
